---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import ScopePanel from '../components/ScopePanel.astro';
import PostLifecycle from '../components/PostLifecycle.astro';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const u = (p: string) => `${rawBase.replace(/\/$/, '')}/${p.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
---

<Base
  title="Post to LinkedIn from your terminal"
  description="postiee posts to LinkedIn from your terminal and keeps a searchable record of everything you posted, because LinkedIn will not let any API read your posts back."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">postiee</p>
    <h1>Post to LinkedIn.<br /><em>Find it again later.</em></h1>
    <p class="hero__sub">
      Post from your terminal. Keep a searchable copy of everything you wrote,
      because LinkedIn won't let any API read your posts back.
    </p>

    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={u('/docs')}>Get started</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="https://github.com/aswathas/postiee" rel="noopener">
        View source
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="stage reveal">
      <Terminal />
      <ScopePanel />
    </div>

    <div class="strip">
      <span>search <b>144 µs</b> / 1k posts</span>
      <span>record <b>1.45 ms</b></span>
      <span>limit <b>150 posts/day</b></span>
      <span>token <b>60 days</b></span>
    </div>

    <div class="install">
      <p class="install__label">macOS · Linux</p>
      <CodeBlock
        code="curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.sh | sh"
      />
      <p class="install__label">Windows · PowerShell</p>
      <CodeBlock
        code="irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aswathas/postiee/main/install.ps1 | iex"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__head reveal">
      <h2>Read that table again</h2>
      <p>Most of it is red. That isn't a gap in the product — it's the reason the product is this shape.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="max-width: 42rem; margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: 0;">
      <p>
        LinkedIn has a permission whose entire job is letting an app read your
        own posts. It isn't slow. It isn't paid. It is <strong>closed</strong> —
        the documentation says it is not accepting access requests at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So you can write to LinkedIn through an approved app, but nothing can
        read back what it wrote. Post something, lose the identifier, and that
        post is gone. Not hidden. Gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why postiee captures the identifier the moment a post is created
        and keeps the record itself. The local history isn't a bonus feature.
        It's the only copy that will ever exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything green in that table is inside the self-serve tier. Everything
        amber or red is either partner-gated or does not exist at any tier. The
        <a href={u('/docs/limits')}>full breakdown is here</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__head reveal">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <p>Four things. No half-finished commands, no stubs that fail on first use.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card reveal">
        <h3>post</h3>
        <p>Write a thought, attach one image, choose who can see it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal">
        <h3>history</h3>
        <p>Every post written to your own database the moment it's made.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal">
        <h3>search</h3>
        <p>Find that thing you posted in March without scrolling LinkedIn.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card reveal">
        <h3>forget</h3>
        <p>Drop a post from your history whenever you want it gone.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__head reveal">
      <h2>The three traps</h2>
      <p>Every one of these breaks a naive LinkedIn client, and all three fail silently.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="max-width: 46rem; margin-inline: auto;">
      <PostLifecycle />
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="max-width: 42rem; margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: 0;">
      <p>
        The post ID arrives in a response header while the body is empty. Person
        IDs are scoped per app, so yours must be resolved at runtime. And URNs in
        paths need full percent-encoding, which Go's <code>PathEscape</code> does
        not do. postiee handles all three, and each has a test.
        <a href={u('/docs/how-it-works')}>How it works</a> walks through them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__head reveal">
      <h2>Your agent already knows how</h2>
      <p>Any agent that can run a shell command can drive it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="max-width: 42rem; margin-inline: auto; padding-inline: 0;">
      <p>
        There's an <code>--agent</code> flag that switches every command to
        clean JSON, an MCP server, and an installable skill for Claude, Codex,
        and Cursor. Point it at whatever you already use and posting stops being
        a separate chore.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div style="max-width: 34rem; margin: 1.5rem auto 0;">
      <CodeBlock code={'postiee post "what I built this week" --image chart.png --alt "signups by week"'} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <h2>Start posting</h2>
    <p>Ten minutes to set up, including your own LinkedIn app.</p>
    <div class="hero__cta">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={u('/docs')}>Read the setup guide</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href={u('/docs/limits')}>See what it can't do</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>